WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.710
What does it take to be a superhero?

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.710
Is it leaping tall buildings with a single
bound, or is it a matter of bravery against

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.950
all odds?

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.539
Maybe you just need a superpower….

00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:13.679
no matter how hilariously terrible that superpower
may actually be.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:17.910
For every all-purpose hero like Iron Man,
there are a hundred lesser-known heroes who'd

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.689
never stand a chance in the Avengers.

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:25.359
Here are just a few of those hundreds of forgotten,
useless heroes and their crappy superpowers.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:26.939
Glob Herman

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.330
For a while, it didn't seem like there was
much of a downside to being a mutant in the

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.439
Marvel universe…

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:36.520
aside from being hated, hunted, and feared
by people who built giant purple murder robots

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.860
made of racism.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.480
Until the early 2000s, mutants were mostly
beautiful.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.350
That all changed when the creative team of
Grant Morrison and Frank Quitely arrived in

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.450
2001 to create New X-Men.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.830
As a result, we ended up with some really,
really ugly messes.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:55.970
One of these misbegotten characters was Glob
Herman, a rotund teenager whose skin was not

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.420
only transparent, but also composed of a substance
like paraffin wax.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.830
Unfortunately for Glob, paraffin wax is pretty
flammable.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.360
That's something that he might've wanted to
consider before joining in with a student

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.690
riot at Xavier's and purposely getting set
on fire.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:15.000
It turns out that being a giant wax-covered
skeleton that's easy to set on fire isn't

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.790
exactly a great asset for the X-Men's action-packed
pursuits.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.860
He survived and, surprisingly, is still kicking
around the Marvel Universe.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.190
Just not with the Human Torch.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.460
Kite Man

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.340
It takes a lot of confidence to commit a crime
in Gotham City.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.520
Odds are that you'll get multiple concussions
from a guy dressed like a spandex vampire

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.560
who's so good at karate they let him into
the Justice League.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.340
But maybe you really think you've got the
edge, whether it comes from your freeze gun,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.370
your fiendishly clever riddles, or, if you're
Charles Brown, your mastery over….

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:46.370
kites.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:51.920
Yes: Kite Man's actual name is Charlie Brown,
named for the comic strip character who famously

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.620
can't keep a kite in the air for more than
two seconds.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:59.530
And if that wasn't insulting enough, the official
guidebook to the DC Universe describes him

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.869
as, quote, "a poor leader and an even poorer
hand-to-hand combatant."

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.159
Ouch.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.170
Kite Man has been revived in modern Batman
comics, getting a new catchphrase — "Kite

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:12.819
Man, hell yeah!" — and a darkly hilariously
origin story that involves his kite-loving

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:14.359
son being poisoned.

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:18.599
None of this makes him better at not getting
punched half to death by Batman and everyone

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:20.040
around him.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.239
Ulysses Solomon Archer

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:26.749
Writer Al Milgrom and artists Herb Trimpe
and Frank Springer were fully aware that they

00:02:26.749 --> 00:02:31.239
were doing something ridiculously goofy when
they put together Marvel's U.S. 1.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:32.239
They had to be.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:37.430
U.S. 1 was, after all, a licensed toy comic
that was meant to tie into a remote-controlled

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.430
big rig.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.510
"How'd you like to get behind the wheel of
a 10-ton truck?

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:42.549
Now you can!"

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:46.840
They did the only thing they could and created
Ulysses Solomon Archer, a kind-hearted trucker

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.870
who got a metal plate in his head after a
crash — and gained the incredible ability

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.540
to telepathically control his 18-wheeler.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.450
He couldn't control every semi truck, just
his.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.079
And he wasn't even the first Marvel hero with
truck powers; the generally-forgotten Razorback

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:03.559
also had a remote-controlled big rig pal.

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:08.439
The weirdest part is that there's one issue
in the comic's 12-issue run that's narrated…

00:03:08.439 --> 00:03:09.879
by the truck.

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:11.689
It's even hinted that the truck might be self-aware.

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:16.250
Still, it's a good enough superpower to give
him occasional cameos in the wider Marvel

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.620
Universe, showing up any time someone needs
a trucker who can drive in outer space.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.709
Which, believe it or not, comes up surprisingly
often.

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:26.120
Hum Dinger

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.059
Spidey Super Stories provided no end of truly
weird, amazing villains for Spider-Man to

00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:32.059
battle.

00:03:32.059 --> 00:03:36.400
It was, after all, a title for young readers
where even characters like Dr. Doom were presented

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.680
as goofballs with wacky schemes… and that's
a guy who skinned his old girlfriend to make

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.180
magical armor.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.590
Case in point: Hum Dinger, a would-be musician
whose bad memory led him to hum onstage.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:51.739
While this initially went over pretty well
with his audience, who were clearly very drunk

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:57.629
or easy to please, his fame quickly went sour,
and he decided to hum for evil.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:03:58.629
That's it.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:02.999
He wasn't, like, supernaturally good at humming
or anything, he was just really, really annoying,

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:05.469
and people gave him money to make him go away.

00:04:05.469 --> 00:04:09.349
It's not exactly the kind of situation that
you'd think would require intervention from

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.930
Spider-Man, but hey, the '70s were a weird
time.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.930
Missing Man

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:18.739
As the co-creator of characters like Spider-Man,
the Question, and Doctor Strange, Steve Ditko's

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:23.669
contributions to the history of comic books
are both important and pretty well-chronicled.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:26.229
His later creations, however, not so much.

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:28.770
Because they got really, really weird.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:34.889
The Missing Man, who made his full debut 1982's
Captain Victory and the Galactic Rangers #6,

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:36.300
is one of those weirdos.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.340
In his handful of increasingly surreal appearances,
his name is variously given as Syd Vane and

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.250
Syd Mane, and he battles crime with the uncanny
power to not have a torso.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:50.790
He's just a pair of spindly arms and a couple
of cartoony legs capped off with an unfinished

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.759
face and big glasses.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.860
If you need more information than that, well...

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:55.860
there isn't any.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.319
It seems that along with a body, the other
big missing piece of Missing Man was any explanation

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:02.819
whatsoever for what his entire deal was.

00:05:02.819 --> 00:05:03.819
Oh, well.

00:05:03.819 --> 00:05:04.819
Thanks for watching!

00:05:04.819 --> 00:05:07.490
Click the Grunge icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.529
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

